// intake.bs -- the boundary, and the one construct that crosses it (F18,
// tickets 11 §2, 15 §2, 27 §8).
//
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Intake Decode "[]"
//         -> []
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Intake Decode "[{ Kind = :'Intake.Reading', Sensor = \"t1\", Value = 21 }]"
//         -> the list, unchanged
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Intake Decode "[{ Kind = :'Intake.Reading', Sensor = \"t1\", Value = :warm }]"
//         -> (:error, (["[0]", ".Value"], "int"))
//     bsc --src-root examples examples/Intake Verdict 7
//         -> :rejected
//
// THE PROPERTY WORTH LOOKING AT is that nothing below says how to walk a list of
// records. `ValidateAs<T>` is a CODEGEN OBLIGATION: the compiler reads the type
// argument, generates a traversal for that one concrete type, and lowers the
// call site to a plain local call of it. There is no `ValidateAs` function in
// the emitted `.beam`, no dispatch on a type at run time, and no type variable
// anywhere -- which is why ticket 27 can hold that this is not a generic call
// even though the language now has real generics.
//
// AND THE FAILURE IS A VALUE, NOT A CRASH. Ticket 15 gave it a payload for a
// reason that is not politeness: `T | :error` COLLAPSES for the very types a
// deep validator is generated over, because a bare atom is absorbed by the atom
// top. The tagged member survives, and it carries a path into the term plus the
// type expected there -- so `Verdict` below can be written at all.
module Intake

record Reading { Sensor: string, Value: int }

// A foreign term arrives as `term` and stays one until something looks (11 §2).
// A clause head may only ask what a BEAM guard decides in O(1), so `list<term>`
// is writable in a head and `list<Reading>` is not -- the traversal is O(n) and
// the SENDER chooses n. That is the whole reason this construct is an explicit
// call at a visible site rather than something a pattern can do.
public result<list<Reading>, ValidationError> Decode(term t)

Decode(t) -> ValidateAs<list<Reading>>(t)

// Reading the result is ordinary branching, because the failure is an ordinary
// value. The path in the error names where the term went wrong and the second
// component names what was expected there -- Gleam's decoder shape, and the
// only precedent that hands a caller something to act on rather than a fact it
// already knew.
public atom Verdict(term t)

Verdict(t) -> Decode(t) switch {
    (:error, e) => :rejected,
    readings    => :accepted
}
